I reconstructed and validated the intended patch to answer the specific questions asked:

1. `inverse` is NOT a bug/omission. In this codebase `littleThan(a, b)` (Axis.ts:339) takes two parameters and closes over `const inverse = axisExtent[0] > axisExtent[1]` (Axis.ts:323); there is no third `inverse` parameter to forget (the 3-arg `littleThan`s live in `src/util/number.ts` and `src/visual/VisualMapping.ts` and are unrelated). Verified empirically with an inversed band axis (extent [300,0]): loops added on top of the existing helper produce [300,200,100,0] — all inside — because the closure handles inversion.

2. Reassigning `last = ticksCoords[ticksCoords.length - 1]` after a pop loop is unsafe as literally described: the index can be -1 (array drained) and `last.coord` then throws, and the reassigned object is a real tick carrying `tickValue`, so the subsequent `clamp` branch mutates a genuine tick's coord out of sync with its `tickValue`.

3. The loops as described (no length guards) crash. With the unguarded variant applied I reproduced `TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'coord')` in three scenarios (ticks entirely right of extent, entirely left of extent, and inversed extent with ticks right of extent).

4. The pop loop can drop a tick that is legitimately inside: the pushed `last` is not guaranteed to be the extreme value (when `diffSize = 1 + dataExtent[1] - lastTickValue` is negative the array is non-monotonic), so testing `ticksCoords[length - 2]` pops the in-extent element first. Concrete: band axis extent [0,300], 5 categories, stale ticks 0..4, `scale.setExtent(-20,-18)` gives coords [2000,2100,2200,2300,2400,300] — the loop pops the only inside coord (300, exactly `axisExtent[1]`) before the outside ones.

5. Completeness: the requirement's own acceptance signal is not actually exercised. Test 3 ("should not overflow the grid when dataZoom is used" — real chart + `dispatchAction({type:'dataZoom'})`) PASSES on unmodified HEAD, i.e. it does not reproduce the bug. I swept ~200 real-chart configurations at baseline (3/5/20/24/60/200 categories, `axisTick.interval` undefined/0/2/3/5/'auto', `alignWithLabel` true/false, `boundaryGap` true/false, `inverse` true/false, showMin/MaxLabel, filterMode filter/weakFilter/empty/none, start/end and startValue/endValue windows, axis min/max, value and time axes, `clamp` true/false) and got 0 overflow cases with no fix at all. The two failing tests only reproduce the overflow by exploiting the per-axis tick cache (`inner(axis).ticks`, src/coord/axisTickLabelBuilder.ts:184-224): they call `getTicksCoords()` once, then mutate `axis.scale.setExtent(2,4)` so cached ticks 0..4 are re-projected onto a narrower scale. In a real chart the coordinate system and its `Axis` instances are rebuilt on every update, so the cache is never stale across a dataZoom action and this path is unreachable. The change is therefore defensive hardening of a synthetic state, and there is no test demonstrating the user-visible "ticks overflow the grid with dataZoom" symptom.

